==============================================================================================================================
1.  THE JEANS MACHINERY, AND THREE ANALYTIC RESULTS IT MUST REPRODUCE BEFORE IT TOUCHES ANY DATA
==============================================================================================================================
  A dispersion is not an acceleration.  Instead of substituting nu into a Newtonian mass estimator, the
  prediction here is sigma itself, from the isotropic spherical Jeans equation
        d(rho_t sigma_r^2)/dr = -rho_t g(r),      sigma_r^2(r) = (1/rho_t) int_r^inf rho_t g dr',
  with rho_t the TRACER (member-galaxy) density and g the framework's own field.  For an isotropic
  population the number-weighted line-of-sight dispersion equals the mass-weighted <sigma_r^2>, so
        sigma_pred^2 = int rho_t sigma_r^2 r^2 dr / int rho_t r^2 dr     over the observed aperture,
  and the liability table's currency follows exactly: boost = g_obs/g_pred = (sigma_obs/sigma_pred)^2.
  tracer models: Plummer (a = r_h/1.3048) and Hernquist-like rho ~ r^-1(r+a)^-3 (a = r_h/2.4134), both solved to the same half-number radius, so they differ only in SHAPE
  [PASS] J1 the Jeans solver reproduces the analytic Newtonian Plummer dispersion, so the integration, the weighting and the units are right   (solver 205.516 km/s vs analytic 205.510 km/s, ratio 1.0000)
  [PASS] J2 the Jeans solver reproduces the deep-MOND virial theorem sigma^4 = (4/81) G M a_0 for an isolated Plummer sphere (Milgrom 1994).  This is the most important validation in the file: it is the exact relation the whole pressure-supported side of the liability table implicitly leans on   (solver gives sigma^4/(G M a_0) = 0.049388 against the exact 4/81 = 0.049383, agreement 0.011%)
  [PASS] J3 in the deep-MOND field of a POINT mass -- which is what a group is, to its satellites -- the tracer dispersion obeys 3 sigma^2 = sqrt(G M a_0) for ANY tracer density profile.  That is why this measurement does not depend on how the satellites are distributed, and it is checked on two profiles differing by a factor 2.4 in central concentration   (Plummer tracers 1.00005, Hernquist-like tracers 1.00005, both against the exact 1)

==============================================================================================================================
2.  THE SAMPLE: Local Volume groups from the UNGC, on the catalogue's own membership criterion
==============================================================================================================================
  UNGC read: 869 Local Volume galaxies (Karachentsev, Makarov & Kaisina 2013)
  distance methods: TRGB=272, TF=204, mem=170, h=86, BS=40, SBF=25
  groups with >= 5 members (host + Theta_1 > 0 satellites): N = 26
  [PASS] S1 the Local Volume supplies a group sample of the size the programme has been assuming it does   (26 groups, 333 member galaxies, host distances 0.01 - 10.70 Mpc)
  [PASS] S2 the groups are dynamically old enough for an equilibrium dispersion to mean anything: the crossing time r_h/sigma must be under a Hubble time, or the Jeans equation does not apply and the measurement is void   (crossing times 0.19 - 12.36 Gyr, median 2.62; longest is NGC4736)
  [PASS] S3 (bug pattern: a residual whose sign tracks a branch of my own prescription) removing the Hubble flow across each group's depth, using the members' individually measured distances, is a REAL choice and it is checked here rather than assumed harmless.  If the correction were large the answer would be a statement about the distance scale, not about gravity   (median |log10(sigma_Hubble-corrected / sigma_raw)| = 0.0122 dex, max 0.1247 dex (NGC6744); gapper vs plain rms differs by 0.0277 dex in the median)

==============================================================================================================================
3.  THE BARYON BUDGET, INCLUDING HOT GAS -- the trap that has bitten every group-scale MOND analysis
==============================================================================================================================
  Lovisari, Reiprich & Schellenberger 2015 (20 X-ray groups, on disk): log M_gas,500 = 1.008 log M500 -1.255, rms 0.091 dex, over M500 = 2.1e+13 - 1.4e+14 Msun; median f_gas,500 = 0.077
  Kravtsov, Vikhlinin & Meshcheryakov 2018 (AstL 44, 8): M_star,500 = 1.7e12 (M500/1e14)^0.6 lets that be
  re-expressed against MEASURED stellar mass and extrapolated down to Local Volume group masses.
      extrapolated X-ray intragroup medium at M_star = 5e+10:  M_hot/M_star = 0.39
      extrapolated X-ray intragroup medium at M_star = 1e+11:  M_hot/M_star = 0.62
      extrapolated X-ray intragroup medium at M_star = 3e+11:  M_hot/M_star = 1.32
  That runs two decades below the fitted range and must NOT be read as a measurement -- and it is known to
  be an over-estimate at the low end, because the fitted slope is near 1 (f_gas roughly constant) whereas
  f_gas is observed to FALL steeply below 10^13 Msun.  The independent handle at this scale is the summed
  circumgalactic medium:
      Bregman et al. 2018 (ApJ 862, 3): Milky Way hot halo ~ 4e10 Msun against M_star = 5e10 -> ~0.8
      Werk et al. 2014 (ApJ 792, 8):    COS-Halos cool CGM ~ 1e10 Msun per L* galaxy         -> ~0.2
  [PASS] B1 the two INDEPENDENT hot-gas prescriptions available at Local Volume group masses -- an X-ray scaling extrapolated two decades down, and a directly measured circumgalactic medium extrapolated across galaxies -- land inside the same bracket, which is what makes f_hot = M_hot/M_star in {0, 0.5, 1.0} defensible rather than invented.  If they disagreed by more than that bracket, the hot gas would have to be the headline uncertainty of this rung and it is not   (X-ray extrapolation gives M_hot/M_star median 0.40 (range 0.05 - 0.71); the circumgalactic measurements give 0.2 - 0.8; the bracket carried is 0 - 1.0 with a central 0.5)
  baryonic masses (stars + HI, no hot gas): log M_b = 9.86 - 11.08, median 10.76; HI is 9% of it in the median, and the host galaxy carries 93% of the stellar light

==============================================================================================================================
4.  THE EXTERNAL FIELD -- and WHICH field it is, which turns out to matter by two orders of magnitude
==============================================================================================================================
  In QUMOND the external-field parameter is e_N = |g_N,ext|/a_0 where g_N,ext is the NEWTONIAN field sourced
  by the actual matter.  In this framework the actual matter is BARYONS.  So the external field is built
  here from the 2MRS K_s luminosities directly.  The 2M++ velocity-field reconstruction is computed beside
  it and is ~100x larger -- because it returns the TOTAL-matter Newtonian field, which in this framework is
  not a Newtonian field at all but (to good accuracy) the MOND field.  Section 4b shows the two agree once
  that is undone, which is the check that decides which number belongs in nu's argument.
  2MRS parsed: 43533 galaxies, 38565 with 350 < cz < 15000 km/s used, log L_K = 8.43 - 12.33

  4b.  THE CONSISTENCY CHECK, at the Local Group where both methods are best calibrated:
      baryonic (2MRS + UNGC):            g_N = 6.024e-15 m/s^2   = 0.00006 a_0
      2M++ LambdaCDM reconstruction:     g   = 1.161e-12 m/s^2   = 0.01240 a_0
      ... read as the framework's MOND field and inverted through nu(y) y a_0 = g:
                                         g_N = 1.422e-14 m/s^2   = 0.00015 a_0
      and the baryonic field, pushed forward through nu, gives a Local Group peculiar velocity of 328 km/s on the crude estimate v ~ g t_0, against the CMB dipole's 620 km/s (the LambdaCDM reconstruction gives 505 km/s the same way).  Neither is a fit; v ~ g t_0 is worth a factor of two and no more, and it is quoted only to show both routes are in the right decade.
      CAVEAT, stated not buried: 2MRS is magnitude-limited and unmasked here for the zone of avoidance, so
      the baryonic sum is a LOWER bound on the external field.  R4 shows a threefold error in it moves the
      median boost by 11%, which is why this does not have to be fixed for the present purpose.
  [PASS] E1 the two external-field routes agree to within a factor 2.5 once the LambdaCDM reconstruction is read as a MOND field and inverted, rather than being fed in as a Newtonian one.  That is what fixes the number going into nu's argument, and it says the naive move -- a LambdaCDM velocity-field reconstruction used directly as g_N -- is wrong by two orders of magnitude in e_N.  It is an agreement between two crude estimates, not a precision result, and R4b carries the consequence of its being wrong   (direct baryonic g_N = 6.024e-15, inverted-from-2M++ g_N = 1.422e-14, ratio 0.424; the un-inverted reconstruction would have given e_N = 0.0124 instead of 0.00006, a factor 193)

