8.  MUTATION CONTROLS
==============================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation -- switch the kernel off (nu = 1, Newtonian gravity on the same measured baryons).  The boost must explode to the dark-matter-to-baryon ratio these groups are famous for; if it did not, the pipeline would not be measuring gravity at all   (Newtonian median boost = 15.0 (range 2.2 - 140.9) against the framework's 0.82: the kernel removes a factor 18 of the Newtonian missing mass in these groups)
  [PASS] M2 mutation -- triple a_0.  In the deep-MOND limit the predicted acceleration goes as sqrt(a_0), so the boost must fall by 1/sqrt(3) = 0.577.  The external field and the transition region push these groups off the pure deep limit, so a 25% departure is allowed but not an arbitrary one   (median boost 0.477 vs 0.817, ratio 0.5841 against the deep-MOND 0.5774)
  [PASS] M3 mutation -- shuffle the measured dispersions across groups.  Every marginal distribution is kept and only the pairing between a group's own baryons and its own kinematics is destroyed.  The MEDIAN boost is a marginal quantity and survives, which is a standing warning about what a median alone can prove; the acceleration slope of R5 must be indistinguishable from the shuffled one, and it is, because there is no slope there to destroy   (shuffled median boost 0.951 vs real 0.817; shuffled slope -0.121 +- 0.148 vs real -0.019)
  [PASS] M4 mutation -- break the groups.  Give every group another group's radius and dispersion while keeping its own baryons.  The result must move well away from the real answer; if a scrambled catalogue gave the same number, the measurement would be a property of the marginal distributions rather than of the systems   (scrambled median boost 1.052 = +0.110 dex from the real 0.817)
  [PASS] M5 both footings, and the answer is not a choice of a_0   (canonical 0.817, alt 0.746, difference +0.040 dex)

==============================================================================================================================
9.  WHAT THIS RUNG SETTLES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
==============================================================================================================================
  MEASURED: 26 Local Volume groups, 333 member galaxies; every
  distance, K_s luminosity and HI mass individually measured; hot gas bracketed by two independent
  prescriptions; the external field built from baryons and cross-checked against the 2M++ reconstruction;
  the prediction made by solving the Jeans equation, not by substituting nu into a Newtonian estimator.
      median boost = 0.82 canonical / 0.75 alt, bootstrap [0.66, 1.11],
      = -0.088 dex, at g_bar/a_0 = 0.0019.

  WHAT IT SETTLES
    1. The cluster deficit does NOT continue downward.  These groups sit at a median 0.0019 a_0 --
       below all 14 X-ray group and cluster rows in the liability table -- and carry a boost of 0.82,
       against those rows' 1.45-3.45.  Whatever the cluster residual is, it is not the low-acceleration tail
       of a kernel error.  The one thing that changes between the two is whether the baryons are COUNTED or
       MODELLED.
    2. The fork gets a SPLIT verdict and STAYS OPEN.  Local Volume groups are pressure-supported and sit
       -0.101 dex from the
       rotating control (-1.24 sigma), so f09's split does not replicate six
       decades of mass away.  On this evidence the split is a property of the dwarf spheroidals rather than
       of pressure support as such -- and the tension is sharp, because the SAME prescription fix pushes
       the dwarf spheroidals the other way, to +0.720 dex (3.5 sigma) from the
       control.  Check F3 records that the two pressure-supported populations disagree by +0.82 dex.
       What that is NOT: a refutation of modified inertia.  Milgrom's theorem
       says only that modified inertia and modified gravity AGREE for circular deep-MOND orbits and DIFFER
       otherwise -- it fixes no sign and no size for the difference on a group's eccentric satellite orbits.
       So this null removes the empirical pattern that motivated the fork; it does not close the fork.
    3. AGAINST INTEREST, and this is the largest single number in the file: the eight classical dwarf
       spheroidals move from f09's +0.228 dex to +0.733 dex once the
       external field is treated as QUMOND requires (Newtonian, baryonic) and the enclosed rather than the
       total mass is used inside the half-light radius.  f09's dwarf spheroidal number should not be quoted
       at its published value.

  WHAT IT DOES NOT SETTLE
    4. It cannot discriminate.  Unity sits inside the bootstrap band [0.66, 1.11] (check R2 FAILS),
       because 5-39 members per group is 0.26 dex on each boost and the sample is 26 groups.  So
       this rung is CONSISTENT with the framework's zero-parameter prediction and must not be quoted as a
       confirmation of it.  LambdaCDM fits these groups too, with haloes.
    5. It rests entirely on E1.  Check R4b fails on purpose: substitute a LambdaCDM velocity-field
       reconstruction for the baryonic Newtonian field in nu's argument and the answer moves into the
       cluster band.  E1's inversion and the Local Group's own motion are what decide that, and they are the
       part of this file most worth attacking.
    6. Membership reaches the zero-velocity surface (Karachentsev's Theta_1 > 0), so infalling galaxies are
       inside the dispersions.  That inflates sigma_obs and therefore inflates the boost, so the measured
       0.82 is an UPPER bound on the true value -- which pushes further from the cluster rows, not
       closer to them.

